  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/b7/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_11.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Peter Parker is brooding over Betty Brant's disappearance when he hears on the radio that Doc Ock is being released from prison - this can't be allowed!&nbsp; But the warden tells him that Octavius has served his time and must be released.&nbsp; But Parker at least creates a spider-shaped tracer that should keep tabs on him.&nbsp; But before he can attach it, he's shocked to see Betty Brant herself picking up Octavius from the prison!&nbsp; He tosses the tracer onto the car and discovers a road map of Philadelphia that fell out of the car.\n\nIn Philadelphia's prison, a man named \"Blackie\" Gaxton (a \"ruthless mobster\") is threatening his lawyer, Bennett Brant, who obviously owes him gambling debt, wanting to know if his sister Betty is doing what he instructed.&nbsp; He will cancel the debt if Dr. Octopus springs him from jail.&nbsp; Betty gave Bennett all her money to pay his debt to Blackie and ran to Philly so Peter wouldn't find out about Bennett.&nbsp; She feels trapped now.\n\nPeter heads to Philadelphia (<i>by jet from NYC we learn - wow, short flight, but I guess they didn't have Uber in those days</i>).&nbsp; He's there to learn the connection between Betty and Doc Ock.&nbsp; His tracer then finds the car, and soon Peter is approaching Betty in a neighborhood.&nbsp; Seeing that he's tracked her down, she feels foolish for running.&nbsp; She then tells him all, that her brother is a lawyer for Blackie, the mobster.&nbsp; Peter indicates that Spider-Man is in town to keep tabs on Doc Ock, and he decides then and there that he's going to tell Betty he's Spider-Man.\n\nMeanwhile, Doc Ock approaches the prison where Blackie is to free him and collect a hundred grand for the job, which will set him up to become the \"king of crime.\"&nbsp; Spider-Man arrives too late to stop the break-out.&nbsp; But his arrival at the prison has given Blackie time to get to Bennett and Betty and kidnap them and head off to a ship to leave the country!&nbsp; But the car they use is the one with the spider-tracer, so Spider-Man is able to track them easily.&nbsp; Bennett has paid his debt to Blackie and pleads to let him and Betty go, but Blackie will hear none of it!\n\nWhen Spider-Man finally swings down, he turns his ankle on a coil of rope.&nbsp; Soon he's led down to face Blackie, Ock and his goons.&nbsp; Octavius says that now he has the money, he doesn't need Blackie anymore.&nbsp; Spider-Man springs into action, pushing some of the goons into Ock, knocking him out of the room, while Blackie grabs his gun and starts shooting at Spider-Man, but when Bennett pushes Betty aside, one of the bullets hits him!&nbsp; He tells Betty it's better this way - \"you can wash your hands...of all this...\"&nbsp; But Betty holds Spider-Man responsible and starts beating on his chest, who can't believe she actually holds him accountable for this!\n\nBut he can't stick around, as both Blackie and Ock are fleeing.&nbsp; He confronts Blackie on deck, livid with what he just did to Betty's brother, but Doc Ock arrives on the scene too, and he has to elude the grasp of those arms, all the while still hampered by the twisted ankle.&nbsp; At the same time, Blackie's goons, seeing their leader is out for the count, grab the one hundred grand for themselves and snatch Betty too for a hostage, but Doc Ock takes their money and Betty, who faints.&nbsp; But when Spider-Man hurls himself at him, he's forced to leave her behind and heads off to a smaller boat, where he figures he'll be at a greater advantage since Spider-Man won't have as much room to escape his arms.\n\nBut the sudden approach of a police launch distracts the two combatants, and their small craft crashes into a piling, hurling them both into the water.&nbsp; By the time Spider-Man surfaces, Dr. Octopus has gotten away.&nbsp; The police re-arrest Blackie and rescue Better as Spider-Man watches.&nbsp; But Ock has escaped.\n\nPeter webs up his ankle and soon joins Betty to comfort her.&nbsp; She realizes that her brother's death wasn't actually Spider-Man's fault, as he was trying to help them, \"But <i>still</i>, I never want to see Spider-Man again!&nbsp; I couldn't bear being reminded...of Bennett!\"\n\nWhile Peter understands, he knows now he can't reveal his secret to her after all.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Once again, a mostly compelling plot - not as engaging as the last issue, as this one focused mainly on a single supporting character - that being Betty, and we only got a very brief glimpse of Aunt May and no others.&nbsp; I did like the fact that while Doc Ock was indeed present, and was even the villain portrayed on the cover, his presence in the story was actually more tied to the plot surrounding Betty than it was for its own purposes.&nbsp; Weaving those two plot elements together like that was a nice touch, and his escape at the end leaves us with another dangling thread that brings us again back to the table for another issue next month!&nbsp; As I've indicated before, the writing on this magazine so far has been hands-down better than any of the other offerings.\n\nI do still wonder how Blackie knew that Ock was about to get out of prison from his Philadelphia cell when Ock was being held in a NYC prison so that he would be available for hire to spring him, but I'll let that curiosity slide.\n\nI have to say that Steve Ditko really does a fantastic \"menacing\" pose for Dr. Octopus.&nbsp; That first panel on page 12 is the best, with the view up toward Ock's face from just below, at the level of his arms.&nbsp; I recall this is the second time he's pulled this off with such skill, as I remember issue #3 had a great look as well.&nbsp; Gil Kane's art too is filled with angles aimed up at characters' heads, which gives them a new dimension, and this reminds me of that here.&nbsp; Well done!&nbsp; Love that panel!\n\nAnd speaking of panels, the last panel of this issue, with Peter walking away into the night is <i>very</i> reminiscent of the final panel of <i>Amazing Fantasy</i> #15, except here Ditko adds the huge, looming figure of Spider-Man to the mix, hovering over Peter in a hauntingly eerie pose that we've all grown so familiar with in the pages of the Spider-Man mags in the decades since.&nbsp; I can't recall for sure, but this may be the first time the pose is captured in the artwork, and it's very effective to show how the specter of Spider-Man hovers over the events of Peter's life.\n\nSo two really great panels here, both in the same issue!&nbsp; Again, well done!\n\nThis is the first appearance of the spider-tracer, and Peter uses a device here to receive the signal, but we know that he's later able to tweak it such that he can pick up the signals using his spider-sense, but once again, it shows how clever he is at equipping himself.&nbsp; His use of the spider-signal in his belt is also featured in this issue to announce his presence before the warden early in the story (which Stan describes as a \"calling card\" in the text).\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME </b>",
  "letters_page": "I loved Joseph Shea's letter -- he obviously <i>hates</i> the notion of the two-part story and says \"If I wanted a continuous story, I would read the comics in the paper -- for free!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=(<i>Hmm</i>, does this kid know that somebody <i>pays</i> for his morning paper every day?)]\n\nChristine Cassello's letter questions Peter being with Betty when she asks \"how old is Jameson's secretary?\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Heh</i>, which is a point I've made myself!)]",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}